  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Just a few showers and isolated thunderstorms \nare expected for tonight. \n\n10 PM Update...\nHaven't changed much in the details. Not much to the cells that\nare out there right now. WPC has mentioned brief heavy rain and\npossibility of some repeat shots of rain in the rough axis where\nthose cells are now (UNV to PHL). But, radar estimates are low.\nCloud tops are not very cold, so they are short cells, but the\nwarm clouds can be the ones that make heavy rain out of smaller\ndroplets. So, we'll not discount the possibility that they could\nyet produce some heavy downpours. However, flooding does not\nappear to be likely at this point. Have nudged temps up a deg or\ntwo due to the decent amt of cloud cover out there. \n\nExpectations are that some additional SHRA/TSRA will develop\nover OH as forcing comes down from lower MI. These will clip or\nget very close to the Laurels overnight. Again, some heavy\ndownpours possible, but the threat is not high enough to warrant\na watch at this point. \n\nPrevious...\nLarge scale uplift is lacking - or even turned negative over the\nregion late this aftn. There are some taller cu in two separate\nlines running NW-SE. We've left PoPs, but not more than 40s, in\nthose areas through 05Z/midnight. But, confidence is low that\nmuch deep convection will develop. With the decided lack of rain\non the regional radar mosaic and only small chances that any \ndevelopment will occur upstream of the flood watch area, we've\ndropped the flood watch. \n\n   -------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Drier, less humid weather returns to most of the\narea Saturday and lasts through Labor Day Weekend.\n\nA cold front draped across the PA/NY border will begin its\nsouthward surge tonight, helped by a deepening upper low over \nNew England. This push of northeasterly winds at the surface\nwill usher in cooler and much less humid air for Saturday and\nthe remainder of Labor Day weekend. There could still be a few\nlingering showers in the southwest part of our area into\nSaturday morning, but coverage and intensity of storms will\ntrend considerably lower. With cool temperatures aloft, we can't\nrule out the chance for an isolated shower or two on Saturday\naround the area along with widespread stratocumulus clouds.\nSaturday will still be a few degrees warmer than average for\nthis time of year, but the real relief arrives by Saturday\nnight. Dewpoints and low temperatures will fall into the upper \n40s and 50s areawide by Sunday morning.\n \nOther than some morning valley fog, the weather will be really \nnice for the Sunday and Labor Day Monday. Highs mainly in the \n70s and lows 45-55F both Sunday night and Monday night will be\nideal for outdoor activities. Little if any rain is expected\nalong with abundant sunshine. At this point, it looks like dry\nweather sticks around through at least midweek. After that, the\nridge may build once again along with the associated chance for\na return to the ring of fire.",
